  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Climate Migration Root Causes in KRI</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Iraq is currently affected by increasing temperatures, reduced and irregular precipitation patterns, soil degradation, flooding and an increased frequency of sand and dust storms. Reduction in rainfall, inefficient usage of water upstream as well as water policies of neighbouring countries have contributed to heightened water scarcity risks across the country. Coupled with rapid urbanization, population growth and environmental degradation, climate change is a worsening threat, impacting human mobility dynamics in the country. In the Kurdistan Region of Iraq (KRI), diminishing agricultural yields and reduced access to household/domestic water are the result of a combination of climatic factors, such as reduced rainfall and drought, and dilapidated water infrastructure, weak governance and policies, historical tribal and land disputes, and broader socio-economic concerns. The combination of these factors continues to create conditions of instability as well as climate-induced human mobility. The nexus between climate change, environmental degradation, human mobility, and security represents a growing concern, but there are only a few integrated interventions to address them. To contribute to the increased resilience of climate change-affected communities in the Kurdistan Region of Iraq as a way to address the adverse drivers of human mobility, IOM will implement a two-pronged approach. Within the first component, IOM will focus on increasing the evidence base on issues linked to climate-induced mobility with a view to supporting the Government of Kurdistan Region of Iraq, local communities and its partners to anticipate risks and manage of climate-induced mobility. In the second component, IOM will support climate-change-affected communities in the Kurdistan Region of Iraq to engage in enhanced local governance structures which support natural resource management and build resilience to the climatic drivers that compel people to move. IOM will use the findings from the first component to inform the operational responses with local communities.</narrative>
  </description>
